If a bit-source is non-junk at depth n+1 and its bit at n is true, the universal neighbourhood of the new code is the intersection of the old neighbourhood with the neighbourhood of the coded Y sequence at n.

The residue of the bit-source modulo two to the power n, plus that power, agrees with the bit-source through bit n, so the state congruence identifies the codes and the twin is non-junk. One inclusion shrinks the new neighbourhood into the old one, and transporting the code to the twin places it in the union that defines the coded sequence. For the other inclusion, a point of the old neighbourhood that lies in the coded sequence lies in some genuine depth-(n+1) atom with bit n forced. If that atom agrees with the bit-source below n, congruence at depth n+1 identifies the codes. If it disagrees, the depth-n neighbourhoods are disjoint, but the point survives in both.
